 ## Background
-Manifold Dual Contouring was proposed by Scott Schaefer, Tao Ju, and Joe Warren [S1]. The manuscript was received on 26 Aug. 2006, accepted on 13 Nov. 2006, and published online on 8 Jan. 2007 in IEEE TVCG [S1][S2]. It is an extension of the original Dual Contouring method by Ju et al. [3], which provides a uniform approach for extracting water-tight isosurfaces on both uniform grids and adaptive octree grids [S1][S2]. The method addresses the issue of non-manifold vertices and edges that can appear in standard Dual Contouring under adaptive simplification [S1][S2]. Ju and Udeshi [28] presented an efficient intersection-free modification to the original DC method by devising simple geometric tests to identify potentially intersecting polygons, which are then tessilated into smaller nonintersecting triangles [S1][S2].
+Manifold Dual Contouring was proposed by Scott Schaefer, Tao Ju, and Joe Warren [S1]. The manuscript was received on 26 Aug. 2006, accepted on 13 Nov. 2006, and published online on 8 Jan. 2007 in IEEE TVCG [S1][S2]. It is an extension of the original Dual Contouring method by Ju et al. [3], which provides a uniform approach for extracting water-tight isosurfaces on both uniform grids and adaptive octree grids [S1][S2]. The method addresses the issue of non-manifold vertices and edges that can appear in standard Dual Contouring under adaptive simplification [S1][S2]. Ju and Udeshi [28] presented an efficient intersection-free modification to the original DC method by devising simple geometric tests to identify potentially intersecting polygons, which are then tessellated into smaller nonintersecting triangles [S1][S2].
 
